:thesis:semantics: <br>
:undisputed:history:
= truth conditional semantics =
====== short ======
Semantic theories based upon the claim that meanings are truth conditions.
now aka *possible worlds semantics*

====== related to ======
hub: [[influential_semantic_theories]]
++[[Fregean_semantics]]
++[[Russellian_semantics]]

= main =
The original naive version of this is sometimes expressed by early analytic
philosophers (such as Russell) as the doctrine that the meaning of a sentence is
its truth conditions. Or, to remain agnostic about the existence of meanings,
sentences are _synonymous_ iff they are true under the same conditions (or in
the same possible worlds).

Consideration of indexicals and the realisation that the truth conditions of
some sentences may vary depending upon their contexts of assertion and
evaluation led to refinement and increased sophistication.

On the modern view:
Assertions (i.e. sentences asserted in a given context of assertion) are
_synonymous_ iff they have the same truth-value with respect to every
circumstance of evaluation.

Or as the [[https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/win2025/entries/meaning/|Stanford Encyclopedia]] somewhat tendentiously puts it:
    two sentences express the same proposition if and only if it is impossible
    for them to differ in truth-value.
(this is a modern version of [[Fregean_semantics#Freges criterion of synonymy]]
hence that can be taken as a form of truth functional semantics - however senses
complicate things)

A function from possible worlds to truth values is sometimes called an
intension in more recent (quasi-technical) literature.

=== objections ===
==== all necessary truths synonymous ====
It seems implausible that all necessary truths assert the same proposition
despite being true in the same possible worlds (i.e. all of them).

e.g. It seems that
    1)    2 + 2 = 4
    2)    There are infinitely many prime numbers
have different meanings despite being true in exactly the same possible worlds
(i.e. all of them).

==== propositional attitudes ====
[[propositional_attitudes]]
If sentence meanings are also the objects of belief (i.e. they are both
propositions) and the sentences (1) and (2) are synonymous, then the belief
ascriptions:
    Mary believes that 2 + 2 = 4
    Mary believes that there are infinitely many prime numbers
should also be synonymous. But it seems possible for them to differ
in truth value and hence they are _not_ synonymous (according to truth
conditional semantics itself).
